{"text":"You are right and I was wrong. I wrote that all three of you had agreed, when all I can honestly say is that each of you told me the will should be understood before anything is settled. That is three separate statements, and I have no business turning them into an agreement. Thank you for correcting me. I will say it that way from now on.

The list now reads as each person's own position. You will not subsidize A's purchase, and you want a fair financial share. You do not know what the will says, who the executor is, or who holds it. A wants to buy the house with her husband at a family discount, and says nothing should move ahead before everyone understands the will and has had a chance to weigh in. B wants money to start a dance studio, and says silence should not count as agreement. My concerns are that we work from the will itself rather than from memory, and that nobody's plan is treated as the front-runner before we know what the will allows.

If none of you holds the will, then finding it comes before any of the money questions. Do you know where your dad kept papers, or which solicitor or bank dealt with his affairs? Those are the usual places to start. I can ask A and B the same, and whoever has it can share it with all three of you at once.","to":"C"}
